/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Charge q is measured in coulombs (C).
   - Power is the rate of energy flow: p = dw/dt = (dw/dq)(dq/dt) = v·i, in watts (W).
   - A DC quantity is constant in time (a 12 V car battery); an AC quantity changes with time, usually as a sinusoid (the 230 V, 50 Hz wall socket).
   - Resistance R is the opposition to current that turns electrical energy into heat.
   - G = 1/R is the conductance in siemens (S); the integrals run from t₀ to t.
   - The energy formulas come from the power: for the inductor p = Li di/dt = d(½Li²)/dt, and for the capacitor p = Cv dv/dt = d(½Cv²)/dt.
   - Because v = L di/dt, a sudden jump in inductor current would need an infinite voltage.
   - Real components are not ideal.
*/
