/* book exam traps: Using v = iR for an inductor or a capacitor. Their laws are v = L di/dt and i = C dv/dt; a constant current gives zero inductor voltage, not iR. | Swapping the two derivative laws: the inductor's voltage depends on di/dt; the capacitor's current depends on dv/dt. | Saying that capacitor current cannot change suddenly. It is the capacitor voltage that is continuous; for the inductor it is the current. | Forgetting the prefix: 100 μF is 100 × 10⁻⁶ F, not 100 × 10⁻³ F; 50 mH is 50 × 10⁻³ H. | Calling ideal L and C lossy. They store and return energy; only resistance (including a coil's winding resistance) turns it into heat. */
