/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An ideal voltage source keeps its terminal voltage at Vs volts for any current; the external circuit decides the current.
   - An ideal current source keeps its current at Is amperes for any terminal voltage; the external circuit decides the voltage.
   - A real battery loses part of its EMF inside itself.
   - A practical current source is an ideal Is in parallel with a resistance Rp.
   - A dependent source has a control (a voltage vx or a current ix measured elsewhere) and an output that behaves as a voltage source or a current source.
   - A dependent source is an active element: it can deliver more power than its control receives, which is what amplification means.
*/
